The patient’s description – extensive, itchy (pruritic) papules with overlying thickening of the nose and forehead – is most suggestive of scleromyxedema. In scleromyxedema, there is a widespread deposition of mucin (and collagen) in the papillary dermis that produces firm, often pruritic papules that can coalesce and lead to a “mask‐like” and thickened appearance of the face. In contrast, scleredema typically manifests as a more homogeneous, indurated thickening of the skin (often on the upper trunk) without a papular pattern, and localized scleroderma (morphea) classically presents with oval, non-pruritic plaques. Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis, by comparison, involves skin thickening that tends to be non-papular and is associated with severe renal failure and gadolinium exposure.

Thus, the features described point most consistently to scleromyxedema.

Final answer: \boxed{A}

